+# Per-plate printers - High Level Design
+
+## Purpose and scope
+
+A project normally slices every plate with the printer and process presets selected in the
+sidebar. Per-plate printers let a plate name its own printer preset, and optionally its own
+process preset, so one project can hold plates for different machines: a plate for a
+256 x 256 mm printer next to a plate for a 180 x 180 mm one, each sliced with its own bed,
+limits, start and end G-code and firmware flavor.
+
+The plate's printer and process are chosen in the Plate Settings dialog ("Printer" and
+"Process"). Choosing "Same as project printer" / "Same as project process" removes the
+override. A plate with its own presets shows the plate settings icon as changed, and its
+tooltip names the plate's printer.
+
+Filaments, the project config (filament colours, flush volumes, ...) and the other plate
+settings stay shared with the rest of the project.
+
+## Data model
+
+`PartPlate` holds a `PlatePresetOverride` (`libslic3r/PlatePresetOverride.hpp`): two preset
+names, empty meaning "the project's". The plate's settings in `PartPlate::m_config` are not used
+for it, because every key there must be a `PrintConfig` option, and a preset name is a project
+reference rather than a print setting.
+
+The override is saved in the plate's metadata in `Metadata/model_settings.config` as
+`orca_printer_preset` and `orca_process_preset`, written only when set. Versions without the
+feature ignore those keys and slice the plate with the project's presets. Only the names are
+saved: a project that names a user preset the receiving machine does not have reports the
+missing preset when that plate is sliced. The names are also part of the plate's undo/redo
+snapshot.
+
+## Slicing
+
+`BackgroundSlicingProcess::apply()` already layers the plate's own settings over the project's
+full config (`PresetBundle::full_config()`). The override is applied at the same point, before
+the plate settings, by `apply_plate_preset_override()`:
+
+- the process preset's `print_options()` are copied over, except keys that are printer,
+  filament or project keys;
+- the printer preset's `printer_options()` are copied over, except project keys;
+- `print_settings_id` / `printer_settings_id` name the plate's presets.
+
+This mirrors the precedence of `PresetBundle::full_fff_config()` (process < filament < printer
+< project), so a plate with its own presets gets the config the project would get with those
+presets selected, for the keys a preset owns.
+
+The full config's per-filament and per-extruder vectors are sized and resolved for the
+project's printer, so the plate's printer must be an FFF printer with the same number of
+extruders and, for a multi-extruder printer, the same extruder variants. The Plate Settings
+dialog only offers printers with the project's extruder count. When an override cannot be
+applied (a missing preset, or a printer that no longer matches after the project's printer
+changed), `apply()` keeps the project's config and remembers the reason, and
+`BackgroundSlicingProcess::validate()` reports it as the plate's slicing error.
+
+`Print::is_BBL_printer()` follows the printer the plate is sliced for.
+
+## Plate layout
+
+Each plate is drawn and checked with its own bed: `PartPlateList::refresh_plate_beds()`
+resolves each plate's printer into a `PlateBed` (printable area, excluded areas, extruder areas,
+printable height, clearances) and `shape_plate()` / `place_plate()` give the plate that shape
+and size instead of the project's. Object containment, the "outside the plate" state,
+arrangement of a single plate and the printable height checks read the plate's own bed.
+
+Plates sit on a grid whose stride is the plate size plus a gap. With every plate on the
+project's bed the stride is the project's plate size, exactly as without the feature. When a
+plate has its own bed, the grid cell becomes the extents of every bed on the grid (the
+project's included), so beds of different sizes or origins never overlap. Changing the cell
+moves each plate, with its objects, to its new place; a bed that changes size re-centers the
+plate's objects, as a printer change does.
+
+`refresh_plate_beds()` runs when a plate's presets change, when the project's bed changes
+(from `set_shapes()`), after a project is loaded and after undo/redo. In the last two the
+objects are already where the saved layout put them, so the plates are moved without them.
+
+## Limitations
+
+- The 3D bed model and axes, the printer logo and the bed type textures belong to the project's
+  printer, and are not drawn on a plate with its own printer.
+- "Arrange all" lays out objects on the project's bed for every plate; arrange a plate with its
+  own printer from its plate toolbar.
+- Device features (sending, printing, AMS mapping, the physical printer) use the project's
+  printer.
+- The command line ignores per-plate presets and slices every plate with the project's presets.
